From b6d15e5d0b9e8bb2425e2fbe639bfabeab2e427b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 20:24:40 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Live Extension actions render as controls in the island

A Live Extension already carried up to three actions across the bus and the
Shell drew none of them.  The island now renders each action as a compact,
icon-only square to the right of the text, and a press calls InvokeAction on
org.projectluma.SemanticBroker1 with the publication that owns the extension,
the extension identifier as the semantic object, and an empty a{sv} parameter,
which is what the interface requires for an action that declares no parameter
type.

The island opens its application from a click gesture that recognizes on press,
so that gesture would always win over a button that only acts on release.  The
gesture now refuses to recognize while the press is inside one of the action
buttons, so the two never fire together.

An action the provider marked disabled stays visible, reads as unavailable,
refuses the press, and carries the provider description as its accessible name
and tooltip.  A control that cannot work for a given application has to say so;
hiding it, or leaving it pressable and inert, would be a lie about what the
system can do.

The Shell chooses the glyph from the action identifier and falls back to a
neutral symbol for vocabulary it does not know, so an application can never
push artwork into panel chrome.  The row is only rebuilt when the actions
themselves change, because a visible timer republishes its extension about once
a second and rebuilding would drop the press under the pointer.
